INFO:beatgen.services.music_agent:Step 'determine_musical_parameters' returning: {
  "chord_progression": "Cm - Ab - Gm - Cm",
  "key": "C",
  "mode": "minor",
  "tempo": 95,
  "melody_instrument_suggestion": "Sampled Synth Bell",
  "chords_instrument_suggestion": "Lush Synth Pad",
  "song_title": "Concrete Reverie",
  "original_user_prompt": "make me a hip hop beat\n\nMusical context information:\n**Genre Classification and Subgenres**\n- **Primary genre:** Hip hop (also known as rap)[5][4].\n- **Major subgenres:** Boom bap, trap, G-funk, East Coast, West Coast, Southern hip hop, lo-fi hip hop, drill, alternative hip hop, jazz rap, mumble rap, and more[1][2][4].\n\n**Typical Tempo Range (BPM)**\n- **General range:** 80–110 BPM for classic and boom bap styles.\n- **Trap and modern styles:** 120–150 BPM is common[2][4].\n\n**Common Instruments and Their Roles**\n- **Drum machines/sampled drums:** Foundation of the beat, providing rhythm and groove (e.g., Roland TR-808, TR-909).\n- **Synthesizers:** Create basslines, melodies, and atmospheric sounds.\n- **Samplers:** Used to chop and rearrange samples from other songs, especially in boom bap and lo-fi styles.\n- **Turntables:** For scratching and DJ effects (especially in classic hip hop).\n- **Bass guitar or synth bass:** Provides low-end support.\n- **Orchestral/other samples:** Horns, strings, or vocal chops for hooks and texture[4][5].\n\n**Notable Reference Tracks**\n- **\"Still D.R.E.\" by Dr. Dre feat. Snoop Dogg** (West Coast/G-funk)\n- **\"Shook Ones, Pt. II\" by Mobb Deep** (East Coast/boom bap)\n- **\"Sicko Mode\" by Travis Scott** (modern/trap)[4][2]\n\n**Production Techniques Common in This Style**\n- **Sampling:** Chopping and reworking segments from older records, especially funk, soul, and jazz.\n- **Layered drum programming:** Combining multiple drum sounds for punch and groove.\n- **Looping:** Repeating short musical phrases to create hypnotic grooves.\n- **808 bass:** Heavy use of sub-bass from the Roland TR-808 drum machine, especially in trap.\n- **Vocal processing:** Use of effects like pitch correction (Auto-Tune), delay, and reverb.\n- **Arrangement:** Emphasis on space for vocals, with instrumental breaks or drops for dynamic contrast[4][2][5].\n\n**Caveats**\n- Hip hop is highly diverse, and specific characteristics can vary widely between subgenres and eras[1][2][4].\n\nChord progression research:\n**Most Common Chord Progressions in Hip Hop (Ranked by Popularity)**\n\n1. **i–VI–v–i** (e.g., A minor – F major – E minor – A minor)\n2. **I–V–IV** (e.g., D major – A major – G major)\n3. **A minor – E minor** (i–v)\n4. **III–i** (e.g., C major – A minor)\n5. **i–VI–VII–iv** (e.g., A minor – F major – G major – E minor)\n6. **i–iv–i–VI** (e.g., E minor – A minor – E minor – C major)\n7. **IV–V–vi–III** (e.g., G major – A major – B minor – F# major)\n\n---\n\n**Details About Why Each Chord Progression Is Used**\n\n- **i–VI–v–i**  \n  - Creates a dark, cyclical, and moody atmosphere, ideal for storytelling and emotional depth in modern hip hop and trap. The use of the minor v chord softens the dominant tension, supporting a chilled vibe[1].\n\n- **I–V–IV**  \n  - Simple, effective, and versatile. This progression is easy to play and provides a strong, familiar foundation for both classic and modern hip hop beats. Its major tonality can be adapted for both upbeat and introspective tracks[2][3].\n\n- **A minor – E minor (i–v)**  \n  - Extremely common for its dark, shadowy quality and simplicity. Works well for looping and provides a solid base for melodic and lyrical development[3][4].\n\n- **III–i**  \n  - Minimalist two-chord progression that keeps the focus on vocals and rhythm. The movement from III to i creates subtle tension and release, making it ideal for hooks and verses[1].\n\n- **i–VI–VII–iv**  \n  - Blends uplifting and melancholic moods, suitable for hip hop ballads and tracks that mix emotional storytelling with energetic beats[1].\n\n- **i–iv–i–VI**  \n  - Popular for its emotional resonance and flexibility. The movement between minor and major chords allows for expressive melodies and is a go-to for many producers[2].\n\n- **IV–V–vi–III**  \n  - Offers a modern, slightly pop-influenced sound with a touch of darkness, making it suitable for crossover hits and melodic rap[2].\n\n---\n\n**References to Specific Tracks Using These Progressions**\n\n- **i–VI–v–i**:  \n  - *“Mask Off” by Future* uses this progression for its iconic, moody trap sound[1].\n\n- **I–V–IV**:  \n  - *“Paper Planes” by M.I.A.* features this classic progression, blending hip hop with pop sensibilities[2][3].\n\n- **A minor – E minor (i–v)**:  \n  - *“Truth Hurts” by Lizzo* employs this simple, effective two-chord loop[3][4].\n\n- **III–i**:  \n  - *“Rich Baby Daddy” by Drake ft. Sexyy Red & SZA* uses this minimalist progression to keep the groove focused on the vocals[1].\n\n- **i–VI–VII–iv**:  \n  - *“Me, Myself & I” by G-Eazy & Bebe Rexha* showcases this emotionally charged progression[1].\n\n- **i–iv–i–VI**:  \n  - *“Kodak Black” by Cardi B* (note: likely refers to “Bodak Yellow” by Cardi B) uses this progression, which is prevalent in current hip hop[2].\n\n---\n\n**Key Points**\n- Hip hop chord progressions are often simple, repetitive, and loop-friendly to keep the focus on rhythm and vocals.\n- Minor keys and modal interchange are common, creating a dark or moody vibe.\n- Producers frequently use chord extensions (7ths, 9ths) and variations to add interest without complicating the core progression[1].",
  "duration_bars": 4
}
